.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_SHUTDOWN 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_shutdown \- stop an event loop's poller and dispatch worker threads
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_event_loop_shutdown(ccol_event_loop loop);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function signals the poller thread of loop to stop. It then
blocks until it joins that thread. With num_reactor_threads > 1, it
then also drains and joins every dispatch worker thread gracefully. A
graceful drain finishes any job that already waits in the queue or
runs, and it cancels nothing. No dispatch can be in flight after this
call returns, in either shape.

This function is idempotent. It is safe to call more than one time,
and safe to call from more than one thread at the same time. Exactly
one caller does the joins. Every other caller blocks until those joins
finish, and only then returns. The "blocks until joined" contract of
each caller therefore holds, whichever caller started the shutdown.

ccol_event_loop_destroy() calls this function itself when the caller
never called it. A direct call first is therefore optional. Make that
direct call when the "the threads stopped" step and the "the library
freed the resources" step must happen at different times.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop  ccol_event_loop to shut down.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success        The function shut the loop down, or the loop was
                    already shut down.
ccol_invalid_args   loop is CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID, or a handle that
                    is stale or that something already destroyed.
ccol_not_permitted  The call comes from inside a callback that runs on
                    one of the threads of loop. That thread is the
                    poller, or a dispatch worker. See WARNINGS.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
Do not call this function from inside a callback that runs on one of
the threads of loop. Such a call joins that thread from itself, which
is undefined behavior and gives EDEADLK. This function joins the
poller thread itself. For num_reactor_threads > 1, it also drains and
joins every dispatch worker thread. In that shape the callbacks run on
a dispatch worker, and not on the poller. The library finds this case
and rejects it with ccol_not_permitted. It does not leave undefined
behavior that a caller can trigger. Give the shutdown to another
thread instead, or do it after the callback returns.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call from more than one thread at the same time. Each such
thread must not be one of the threads of loop. Those threads are the
poller thread, and, for num_reactor_threads > 1, the dispatch worker
threads.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_event_loop_destroy (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_create (3)
